[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviours the translation had to get right (checked, all correct)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are the places where a plausible translation would have diverged. Each is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m noted here because a passing test is only meaningful if the reason it passes is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m understood.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **`x` survives a failed `scanf`.** `int x = 0;` is the value used when the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    conversion fails or hits EOF; the C code never checks `scanf`'s return value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    The Rust `main` mirrors this by only overwriting `x` on success. A translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    that treated a parse failure as an error exit would differ in exit status on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `""` and `"abc"` while still matching stdout on the happy path — which is why
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the suite asserts the exit status on every case.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **Overflow saturates in `long`, then narrows to `int`.** glibc's `%d` goes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    through `strtol`, so `2147483648` becomes `(int)2147483648L` = `-2147483648`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (`00000080…`), whereas `9223372036854775808` saturates to `LONG_MAX` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    narrows to `-1` (`ffffffff…`). Saturating at `INT_MAX`, or wrapping instead of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    saturating, would both pass the small-value tests and fail here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **`scanf` crosses newlines.** `%d` skips any run of whitespace, newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    included, so `"\n\n  \t\n 42"` yields `42`. An `fgets`/`read_line`-based
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    translation would stop at the first newline and produce `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **Struct object representation.** `house_t` is `{int, int, double}`: 16 bytes,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    8-byte aligned, with no padding holes on the LP64 little-endian target, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `memcpy` out of it is `floors` LE, `bedrooms` LE (`03000000`), then `2.0` as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    little-endian IEEE-754 (`0000000000000040`). Getting the endianness or the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    field order wrong would change every byte of output. The suite pins the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    output length at 33 bytes so a wrong `sizeof` cannot pass silently.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **`print_hex` formatting.** `%02x` on `unsigned char`, no separators, one
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    trailing `\n`, and nothing on stderr. Compared byte for byte.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **Exit status is always 0** and stderr is always empty, for every input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    including binary garbage and a failing stdout.
